 61CR c  {+61}V produced in fragmentation of {+76}Ge{+30+} beam on a {+58}Ni    
 61CR2c  target.  LISE3 achromatic spectrometer used to separate fragments;     
 61CR3c  magnetic rigidity was tuned to optimize transmission of {+62}V         
 61CR4c  {+64}Cr fragments.  Transmitted nuclei were identified by three        
 61CR5c  consecutive Si detectors where two served for energy loss and          
 61CR6c  time-of-flight measurements while the last determined their residual   
 61CR7c  energies.                                                              
 61CR c  Measured E|g, I|g, I|b, |g|g, |b|g coin, |g(t), lifetimes with four Ge 
 61CR2c  detectors placed around a thick Si telescope.  Half-lives determined by
 61CR5c  fitting procedures involving five parameters: half-lives of mother,    
 61CR6c  daughter and grand-daughter nuclei, the |b-efficiency and the          
 61CR7c  background rate over the 1 s collecting time.                          
 61CR c  Due to the lack of observation of |g|g coincidences, the level scheme  
 61CR2c  given is tentative.                                                    
 61CR cB IB$Sum of all detected |g-transitions in 2005Ga01 amounts to 35% {I10} 
 61CR2cB of the |b strength. The authors attribute missing strength as either   
 61CR3cB feeding the ground state or high-energy states. They note that given   
 61CR4cB the present |b-|g efficiency as a function of photon energy and the    
 61CR5cB number of implanted nuclei, that 2.5 MeV |g rays originating from      
 61CR6cB levels fed by I|<10% would not have been seen. The upper limit would   
 61CR7cB increase if high-energy states in {+61}Cr decay through several        
 61CR8cB |g-transitions with weak intensity for each. Shell model               
 61CR9cB calculations predict that |b-strength fragmented over 10 levels in 61Cr
 61CRXcB with |?10% intensity for each of the 10 levels.                        
